battlefield hardline turns out it's not  just a perpetual source of leaks but an  honest to goodness video game and you  know how I know that because I actually  got to play a couple hours of  multiplayer and it's pretty fun I mean  yes it's definitely a battlefield game  but it's also got some interesting  things going on first  there's the map design the one I played  on was a downtown Los Angeles map called  high tension and it's not the biggest  map in the world but it's definitely got  the most verticality to it in terms of  battlefield there's just so many layers  that you've got overpasses underpasses  multi-story parking garages skyscraper  rooftops and getting around all this  stuff with the new grapple hook and  zipline gadgets it's actually really fun  to items that work across all four  classes the grapple hook lets you climb  ledges where there's no ladder while the  zipline lets you quickly traverse wide  gaps without the risk of tumbling to a  grisly death if you're playing a game  mode like Blood Money where you're  running around with a bright red duffel  bag full of cash strapped to your back  avoiding surface streets by ziplining  between buildings can be a pretty  effective tactic it's also just fun  because it's a freakin zipline next up  vehicles they're still here it's a  battlefield game so there's vehicles one  difference though is that they feel a  bit more forgiving take the motorcycle  for example not only can you bash  through guardrails like it was nothing  you can also do these little wall rides  if you get close enough to a vertical  surface bigger vehicles seem a little  more durable to at least in so much as  they don't explode on impact if you  collide into something at more than 10  miles an hour plus there are special  vehicles that act as mobile spawn points  which is pretty cool but one word of  warning it might take a little while to  get used to the fact that all those  other vehicles parked around downtown LA  are just for show you can't get in them  don't even think about it so what about  the game modes what's new about those  well I got to play two of them heist and  blood money to be honest heist feels  pretty similar to rush at least in the  way in an attacking team has to crack  two vaults while the defenders try to  stop them the difference being that once  those vaults are cracked  the attackers then have to transport a  pair of briefcases to two different  escape  so there are some minor differences but  the whole flow doesn't really feel too  different from Rush now Blood Money is  different this mode is really cool and  seems to do a great job of tapping into  the whole cops-and-robbers setting  basically there's this pile of cash in  the middle of the map and both teams try  to take that money back to their own  armored trucks the thing is you can skip  the cash pile and go straight to the  other team's truck so there's a whole  lot of back-and-forth going on it's one  of those modes where you're constantly  weighing risks do I take the time to  grab another hundred thousand dollars  even though I hear enemy footsteps or in  another situation do I help a teammate  getting shot up even though I'm loaded  with cash and it's all made better by  the fact that you're not powerless when  you're carrying around that money you  can run at normal speeds and drive  vehicles it all feels like a bigger kind  of sloppier version of that famous  shootout scene from heat and that's hard  lines multiplayer in a nutshell there  are some fun new tweaks but you know for  better or worse it's still very much a  battlefield game want to know more well  be sure to head back to GameSpot in the  near future for more details on  everything from class differences to the  progression system - stay tuned
 

